<article class="env-exercise" id="02CN">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02CN">Exercise <span data-tag="02CN">111.1.8</span>.</a> Let $\nu : k[x]\setminus \{ 0\}  \to {\mathbf Z}$ be a map with the following properties: $\nu (fg) = \nu (f) + \nu (g)$ whenever $f$, $g$ not zero, and $\nu (f + g) \geq min(\nu (f), \nu (g))$ whenever $f$, $g$, $f + g$ are not zero, and $\nu (c) = 0$ for all $c\in k^*$. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
Show that if $f$, $g$, and $f + g$ are nonzero and $\nu (f) \not= \nu (g)$ then we have equality $\nu (f + g) = min(\nu (f), \nu (g))$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Show that if $f = \sum a_ i x^ i$, $f\not= 0$, then $\nu (f) \geq min(\{ i\nu (x)\} _{a_ i\not= 0})$. When does equality hold? 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Show that if $\nu $ attains a negative value then $\nu (f) = -n \deg (f)$ for some $n\in {\mathbf N}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Suppose $\nu (x) \geq 0$. Show that $\{ f \mid f = 0, \  or\  \nu (f) > 0\} $ is a prime ideal of $k[x]$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Describe all possible $\nu $. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>